/*
    To convert the Semi-Formalized Structure into a Formalized Structure, we will examine the natural language clauses in the Semi-Formalized Structure one by one, and formalize each into a formal clause that is well-formed and well-defined according to the guidelines.
*/

{
    "premises": [
        /*
        The clause "The distinct points U, V, and X form a triangle △ U V X" has a potential direct formalization in the guidelines: `formTriangle`.

        We double check the description of the `formTriangle` relation: "mutually distinct points a, b and c form a triangle, where point a and b are on line AB, point b and c are on line BC, point a and c are on line CA. The lines AB, BC, and CA must be distinct".

        The mutual distinctness of points in our target clause matches the description of "mutually distinct points a, b and c". By definition, the 3 points that form a triangle must be non-collinear, which matches the description "The lines AB, BC, and CA must be distinct". Therefore, this relation is indeed equivalent to our target clause.

        `formTriangle` takes (a b c : Point) (AB BC CA : Line) as arguments in order, so we can formalize the clause as: `formTriangle U V X UV VX XU`.

        The following clause "The distinct points V, W, and Y form a triangle △ V W Y" can be formalized in the exact same way.

        Therefore, we can formalize the first 2 clauses as follows:
        */
        "formTriangle U V X UV VX XU",
        "formTriangle V W Y VW WY YV",

        /*
        The clause "The distinct points U, V, and W are collinear" doesn't have any potential direct formalization in the guidelines, so we have to formalize it equivalently using available operators, constants, and other formal relations.

        We notice that this clause is equivalent to "U and W are distinct points on line UW, V and W are distinct points on line UW, and V and U are distinct points on line UW", which has a potential direct formalization in the guidelines: `distinctPointsOnLine`.

        We double check the description of the `distinctPointsOnLine` relation: "points a and b are distinct and on line L". It is indeed equivalent to our target clause since it guarantees that the points are distinct.

        It takes (a b : Point) (L : Line) as arguments in order, so we can formalize the clause as follows:
        */
        "distinctPointsOnLine U W UW ∧ distinctPointsOnLine V W UW ∧ distinctPointsOnLine V U UW",

        /*
        The clause "The distinct points X and Y are on the same side of line UW, both not on line UW" has a potential direct formalization in the guidelines: `sameSide`.

        We double check the description of the `sameSide` relation: "point a and b are on the same side of line L. They both are not on line L, but can be the same point". It is not exactly equivalent to our target clause since it does not guarantee that the points are distinct, but we can still use it to formalize the clause by adding an additional clause `X ≠ Y` to guarantee distinctness.

        The `sameSide` relation is defined in the namespace `Point` with the syntax `a.sameSide b L`, so we can formalize the clause as follows:
        */
        "X.sameSide Y UW ∧ X ≠ Y",

        /*
        The clause "The line segments WY and VX are congruent, denoted as W Y ≅ V X" doesn't have any potential direct formalization in the guidelines, so we have to formalize it equivalently using available operators, constants, and other formal relations.

        The definition of congruence of line segments is that the length of the line segments are equal.

        We notice that, following the instructions, the formal notation of a line segment fromed by points a and b is `|(a─b)|`, so we can directly formalize the clause using the equality relation `=` as: `|(W─Y)| = |(V─X)|`.

        The following clause "The line segments VY and UX are congruent, denoted as V Y ≅ U X" can be formalized in the exact same way.

        Therefore, we can formalize the next 2 clauses as follows:
        */
        "|(W─Y)| = |(V─X)|",
        "|(V─Y)| = |(U─X)|",

        /*
        The clause "V is the midpoint of line segment UW" doesn't have any potential direct formalization in the guidelines, so we have to formalize it equivalently using available operators, constants, and other formal relations.

        The definition of the midpoint of a line segment is the point in between and equidistant from the endpoints of the segment. In our case, "V is the midpoint of line segment UW" means that "V lies between U and W on the same line, and V is equidistant from U and W".

        We notice that the between relation has a potential direct formalization in the guidelines: `between`.

        We double check the description of the `between` relation: "mutually distinct points a, b and c are collinear and ordered. Point b is between point a and c". Since we know that U, V, W are mutually distinct, this relation is indeed equivalent to our target clause.

        It takes (a b c : Point) as arguments in order, with Point b being the one that is between Point a and Point c, so we can formalize this part as: `between U V W`.

        Also, we notice that we can directly formalize the equidistance  using the length of the subsegments of UW, denoted respectively as `|(U─V)|` and `|(V─W)|`, and relating them using the equality relation `=`: `|(U─V)| = |(V─W)|`.

        Finally, we can formalize the clause as the logical conjunction of the two conditions:
        */
        "between U V W ∧ |(U─V)| = |(V─W)|"
    ],
    "conclusions": [
        /*
        The clause "The triangles △ V W Y and △ U V X are congruent, denoted as △ V W Y ≅ △ U V X" has a potential direct formalization in the guidelines: `congruent`.

        We double check the description of the `congruent` relation: "triangle T1 and T2 are congruent. Note that the order of vertices of T1 and T2 must correspond to each other. For example, if T1 is △ A:B:C, then T2 must be △ D:E:F with A corresponding to D, B corresponding to E, and C corresponding to F". It is indeed equivalent to our target clause.

        We check the point V corresponds to the point U, the point W corresponds to the point V, and the point Y corresponds to the point X.

        The `congruent` relation is defined in the namespace `Triangle` with the syntax `T1.congruent T2`. Following the instructions, the formal notation of a triangle formed by points a, b and c is `△ a:b:c`, so we can formalize the clause as follows:
        */
        "(△ V:W:Y).congruent (△ U:V:X)"
    ]
}
